## Verification Criteria (what a tester will CONFIRM — your change must satisfy every one)
These are observable checks, derived from the acceptance criteria and description. They describe WHAT is observed, not how to build it. Make the minimal change that makes all of these true; your accompanying test should assert them:
- When a page is loaded normally (without an active CMS editing session), all content on the page displays the published/default values as before
- When a CMS editor changes the value of a content field in a draft entry, the rendered page updates to display the new field value without requiring a manual page reload
- When a CMS editor modifies multiple content fields across different entries simultaneously, each corresponding area of the rendered page reflects its updated value
- When a CMS editor stops the editing session or navigates away, the rendered page continues to display the last-rendered content without errors or blank areas
- When a CMS editor changes a content field and then reverts it back to the original published value, the rendered page updates to show the reverted value

